\subsection{Invalidate a Cached Answer That Found No Binding}

A reused query needs evidence for absence as well as presence.
Consider lexical name resolution over an immutable revision with
an acyclic inner-to-outer scope chain. There is at most one
declaration per spelling in each scope, with no overloads or
implicit imports. Give each scope a stable identity, a parent key,
and one fingerprinted binding bucket per name, including an explicit
missing token. The lookup key names the starting scope and
spelling. A successful output here is just a declaration identity,
not a type or body. Reading a bucket and resolving that
declaration's signature are different queries.

\begin{sudocode}[Record the search, including empty buckets]
\State begin a fresh dependency record
\State $s\gets$ starting scope
\While{$s$ exists}
  \State $b\gets\Call{ReadRecording}{bucket(s,name)}$
    \Comment{An empty bucket is a dependency}
  \If{$b$ contains a declaration}
    \State \Return its stable identity with recorded reads
  \EndIf
  \State $s\gets\Call{ReadRecording}{parent(s)}$
    \Comment{Scope reparenting changes the search}
\EndWhile
\State \Return not found with recorded reads
\end{sudocode}

Let scope 12 have parent 5. Neither scope binds \texttt{rate},
so a query for \texttt{rate} returns not found. Add a declaration
with identity 91 to scope 5. The fingerprint of its formerly
empty bucket changes, and rerunning returns 91. Then insert
a shadowing declaration with identity 47 in scope 12; the
query returns 47 and stops reading scope 5. An edit of an
unrelated spelling such as \texttt{unit} need not invalidate
any of these answers. Test these edits against a cache-free
scope walk, including deletion of the shadowing declaration.

If all recorded inputs agree, the deterministic walk visits
the same scopes, in the same order, and returns the same
answer. A cache that records only the final declaration would
miss both the insertion after failure and later shadowing.
Using whole-scope fingerprints would be sound here but
more eager to invalidate. Granular buckets let unrelated
changes preserve useful work.

\section{Reusable bytes are not an installed activation}

Mehta and colleagues' \emph{Reusing Just-in-Time Compiled Code} indexes compiled R function versions by their specialization context and curates a persistent repository. Contextual dispatch selects a candidate without making the observed context a universal property of the function. Park and colleagues' \emph{Reusing the Optimized Code for JavaScript Ahead-of-Time Compilation} addresses a second problem: optimized machine code may contain process-specific addresses, so reuse includes relocation and handling profile-based specialization. Neither mechanism can be reduced to comparing source checksums. Include target features, backend and stencil version, VM ABI, relocation schema, and relevant semantic dependencies in reuse criteria. A profile may rank several valid artifacts; if the pipeline consumed profile data to produce an exact artifact, include the appropriate profile fingerprint in the reproducibility key. Guard and dependency validity still must be checked at installation and where required during execution.

Dossier Chapter~44 proposes hermetic compilation capsules; Chapter~21 discusses reachability-based retirement. A plausible capsule stores a reproducibility manifest, relocatable code, recovery metadata and the verdicts of the checks actually run. Hydration gives the artifact a new in-process version identity and fixes process-local relocations before publication. The capsule cannot promise exact output bytes if it omits inputs such as layout randomization, nor can a digest alone justify semantic reuse. Replay the edit sequence with the cache disabled, then enabled; compare results, exceptions and observations, and report memo hits, recompiled functions, hydration cost and bytes held by old frames separately (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-255}).
